Knots-style relay policy for Bitcoin Core, for nodes staying on SHA256.

Against Bitcoin Core v31.1.0.

WHY THIS EXISTS
===============

Bitcoin Knots carries a set of relay-policy options that keep data carriers
out of the mempool: -rejectparasites, -rejecttokens, -permitbarepubkey,
-permitbaredatacarrier, -datacarriercost, -acceptnonstddatacarrier,
-maxscriptsize. Core has none of them.

Since 30 August 2026 Knots releases follow the BLAKE2b chain that split at
961,640. Operators who wanted that relay policy but intend to stay on SHA256
therefore have no software: Core follows their chain and lacks the policy,
Knots has the policy and (at minimum, ambiguously) does not follow their
chain.

This patch puts the policy on Core. It is relay policy only. It changes no
consensus rule, rejects no block, and cannot fork the chain. A node running
this accepts every block a stock Core node accepts. The most it can do is
decline to *relay* a transaction, and if that transaction is mined, this
node accepts the block containing it like any other.

That boundary is the entire safety argument, and it is why the changes live
in src/policy/ and touch no validation code.

STATUS — READ THIS BEFORE BUILDING
==================================

NOT COMPILED. NOT TESTED. I have no Core build environment and did not
compile this. Treat it as a reviewed design, not a working patch. Line
offsets against v31.1.0 are likely to need adjustment.

Before it goes anywhere near mainnet:

  1. It must compile.
  2. test/functional/feature_spam_policy.py must pass.
  3. Run it on regtest and confirm a stock Core node and a patched one
     accept exactly the same BLOCKS while differing on mempool admission.
     That test is the one that proves the consensus claim above.

Step 3 is not optional. The claim "policy only, never consensus" is the
whole product, and an untested claim of that shape is worse than no claim.

